---
title: "Recipe: a figure from a CSV"
---

The canonical data-figure loop: read a CSV, plot it, and label the cell so the
figure is numbered and cross-referenceable. The `{python}` cell below reads
`data.csv` (sitting next to this document) and renders its plot inline when you
preview with a kernel. A `#| label:` with a `fig-` prefix makes the output a
numbered figure, and `#| fig-cap:` is its caption, so `@fig-sales` resolves on
its own.

`#| cache: false` is the honest setting for this one cell. The freeze cache keys on
the cell's code plus everything upstream of it, and `data.csv` is neither: editing the
CSV without touching the document would replay a stale figure from `_freeze/` forever.
A cell whose real input sits outside the document opts out.

```{python}
#| label: fig-sales
#| fig-cap: "Monthly sales from `data.csv`."
#| cache: false
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

data = pd.read_csv("data.csv")
fig, ax = plt.subplots()
ax.plot(data["month"], data["sales"], marker="o")
ax.set_xlabel("month")
ax.set_ylabel("sales")
```

@fig-sales shows the trend. Swap `{python}` + pandas for `{r}` + readr
(`read_csv("data.csv")`) to do the same against the R kernel: the `#| label:`
and `@fig-` reference are identical.
